/*
xlsxt.lcb - xTalk Builder (LCB) binding library for Excel .xlsx files, via
            the xlsx_shim C shim. Part of xlsxt for OpenXTalk (OXT).

Once this extension is loaded, every public handler below is callable
directly from xTalk script, e.g.
    put xlWorkbookNew(specialFolderPath("documents") & "/report.xlsx") into sBook

Conventions (the shim, src/xlsx_shim.c, is where each is enforced):
  * Handles are integers (0 = invalid). Every handler tolerates a stale or 0
    handle: getters return 0 or empty, actions do nothing. A handle of one
    kind (a sheet) is refused where another kind (a format) is expected.
  * NAMES ARE MECHANICAL: the handler for the shim's xlx_sheet_write_number
    is xlSheetWriteNumber; nothing is renamed by hand.
    tools/check-lcb-signatures.py holds the bijection (every export bound
    once, every binding wrapped by the one public handler of that name).
  * Rows, columns, sheets and series are 1-BASED, as chunks are in xTalk:
    row 1, column 1 is cell A1.
  * Text goes IN as UTF-8 (ZStringUTF8) and comes OUT as a Pointer that
    textOf() copies, never as ZStringNative.
  * A yes/no crosses the public boundary as a Boolean and the C boundary as
    an int (asInt() below; `tR is not 0` on the way back).
  * Nothing here throws, bar the ABI check: a refused call returns 0 or empty
    and xlLastError() says why. A loop writing ten thousand rows must not be
    stopped by one bad cell; xlWorkbookErrorCount says afterwards whether
    every call was taken.

The foreign handlers bind to the shared library "xlsxt" (built by
CMakeLists.txt). Bindings resolve on first use, so the library only needs to
be present at run time.
*/

/**
Reads and writes Excel workbooks (.xlsx files), with no copy of Excel and
no other program needed.

To WRITE a file, make a workbook with <xlWorkbookNew>, give it sheets with
<xlWorkbookAddSheet>, put values into cells with <xlSheetWriteText>,
<xlSheetWriteNumber>, <xlSheetWriteFormula> and the rest (or a whole table
at once with <xlSheetWriteGrid>), and finish with <xlWorkbookClose>, which
is the call that writes the file. Formats from <xlWorkbookAddFormat> give
cells their fonts, colours, borders and number formats; charts from
<xlWorkbookAddChart> draw the numbers.

To READ a file, open it with <xlReadOpen>, ask what it holds with
<xlReadSheetCount> and <xlReadSheetName>, read cells with <xlReadCell> or a
whole block with <xlReadGrid>, and finish with <xlReadClose>. Files written
by Excel, LibreOffice, Numbers and Google Sheets can be read.

A workbook that is being written cannot be read back or changed until it is
closed, and an existing file cannot be edited in place: read it, then write
a new one.

Rows and columns are numbers starting at 1, so row 1, column 1 is cell A1;
<xlCellName> and <xlColumnName> turn numbers into Excel's letters. Workbooks,
sheets, formats, charts and readers are integer handles. 0 means a call was
refused, and a stale handle is harmless: getters return 0 or empty, actions
do nothing. <xlLastError> says why a call was refused, and
<xlWorkbookErrorCount> says how many calls a workbook has refused so far.

A date is a number to Excel: the days since the end of 1899, with the time
as a fraction. <xlDateToSerial> and <xlSerialToDate> convert, and a cell
shows as a date when its format has a date number format.

Built on libxlsxwriter by John McNamara, with zlib and Expat.

Tags: Spreadsheet, Excel, Files, Library
*/
library org.openxtalk.library.xlsxt

use com.livecode.foreign

-- The engine's own string builder, resolved by NAME (so no leading
-- underscore): see textOf() below.
foreign handler MCStringCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pByteCount as CUInt, in pEncoding as CUInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

metadata title is "Excel Spreadsheets for OpenXTalk"
metadata author is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata version is "0.1.0"
metadata summary is "Reads and writes Excel .xlsx workbooks (cells, formats, formulas, charts, images) via libxlsxwriter."

